const TOPICS_SPEC = [
{id:"aml",track:"legal",title:"AML, KYC & sanctions (financial crime compliance)",short:"How banks and fintechs stop money laundering: customer checks, screening, monitoring and reporting",weeks:"Spec 1",prio:true,sk:["aml","finreg"],
 obj:"Explain money laundering and India's PMLA framework, perform KYC and customer due diligence, handle sanctions and PEP screening, investigate monitoring alerts, and know when and how suspicious activity is reported.",
 why:"KYC and AML analyst roles are among the largest entry-level compliance job families in India, at banks' global centres (Citi, JPMorgan, Barclays, HSBC), Indian banks, fintechs and consulting firms. Postings ask for CDD/EDD, PEP and sanctions screening, adverse media checks, quality scores and escalation, and value certifications such as ACAMS CAMS. Your US credit-file and documentation experience transfers directly.",
 subs:[
  {t:"Money laundering and terrorist financing",l:1,b:"Laundering hides the criminal origin of money in three stages: placement (getting cash into the system), layering (moving it through transactions to blur the trail) and integration (using it as clean money). Terrorist financing can use clean money for criminal ends. Banks and other 'reporting entities' are gatekeepers because they see the transactions.",ex:"Cash from fraud is deposited in many small amounts across mule accounts, moved through a shell company, then used to buy property.",v:"money laundering stages placement layering integration explained"},
  {t:"India's law: PMLA 2002 and the PML Rules",l:1,b:"The Prevention of Money-laundering Act, 2002 and the PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005 apply to reporting entities: banks, NBFCs, payment system operators, securities and insurance intermediaries, virtual digital asset service providers and certain professionals. Core duties: customer due diligence, record keeping (generally five years), reporting to the Financial Intelligence Unit (FIU-IND), and a principal officer and designated director. The Enforcement Directorate investigates offences.",v:"PMLA 2002 explained reporting entity obligations"},
  {t:"KYC and customer due diligence",l:1,b:"KYC confirms who the customer is and what they will use the account for. RBI's KYC Master Direction sets the documents (officially valid documents such as passport or Aadhaar with proper safeguards), video-based KYC, the Central KYC Records Registry (CKYCR), risk categorisation, and periodic updation (in RBI's framework: high risk every 2 years, medium every 8, low every 10). Every check must be documented so a reviewer can follow it.",v:"RBI KYC master direction explained CDD"},
  {t:"Beneficial ownership, PEPs and enhanced due diligence",l:1,b:"For companies, the beneficial owner is the natural person who ultimately owns or controls the client; India lowered the ownership threshold for companies from 25% to 10% in March 2023. Politically exposed persons (PEPs), complex structures, high-risk countries and unusual source of funds trigger enhanced due diligence (EDD): source of wealth, senior approval, adverse media searches and closer monitoring.",v:"beneficial ownership PEP enhanced due diligence explained"},
  {t:"Sanctions screening",l:1,b:"Customers and payments are screened against lists such as UN Security Council sanctions, orders under Section 51A of India's UAPA, and, for US-linked business, OFAC lists (plus EU and UK lists where relevant). Screening tools use fuzzy matching, so most hits are false positives that analysts clear with identifiers (date of birth, nationality). A true match means freezing or blocking and escalating, never tipping off the customer.",v:"sanctions screening false positive true match analyst"},
  {t:"Transaction monitoring and alert investigation",l:1,b:"Monitoring rules flag patterns such as structuring below thresholds, rapid in-and-out movement, many unrelated senders (mule accounts) or activity inconsistent with the profile. The analyst reviews the alert, gathers context (KYC profile, history, counterparties), and closes it with a written rationale or escalates it. Narratives must answer who, what, when, where, why it is unusual and what was checked.",ex:"Alert: a student account received 46 UPI credits from different people in 3 days and moved 95% out to one wallet. Profile income: none. Escalate as possible mule account.",v:"AML alert investigation narrative writing transaction monitoring"},
  {t:"Reporting to FIU-IND",l:2,b:"Reporting entities file suspicious transaction reports (STRs) through FIU-IND's FINnet systems. A 2023 amendment to the PML Rules requires suspicious transaction information to be furnished promptly, replacing the earlier seven-working-day period; FIU guidance still describes other reports, such as cash transaction reports, as due monthly by the 15th of the following month. Tipping off the customer is prohibited.",v:"FIU-IND STR reporting process India"},
  {t:"The global view: FATF and US BSA",l:2,b:"The Financial Action Task Force (FATF) sets 40 Recommendations and evaluates countries; India's 2024 mutual evaluation placed it in 'regular follow-up', the best outcome category. Many Indian centres support US banks, where the Bank Secrecy Act requires suspicious activity reports (SARs) to FinCEN, generally within 30 days of detection.",v:"FATF 40 recommendations mutual evaluation explained"},
  {t:"Tools of the trade",l:2,b:"Screening databases (LSEG World-Check, Dow Jones Risk & Compliance, LexisNexis Bridger), monitoring and case platforms (NICE Actimize, Oracle FCCM, SAS AML), internal KYC systems and Excel for QC sampling. Postings rarely require tool experience for juniors but list it as an advantage.",v:"World-Check Actimize AML tools overview"},
  {t:"Certifications and career path",l:3,b:"ACAMS CKYCA (Certified Know Your Customer Associate) suits beginners; ACAMS CAMS is the best-known AML credential and uses an eligibility credit system. The Indian Institute of Banking & Finance also runs AML/KYC courses; check its current list. Path: KYC analyst, AML investigator, QC or team lead, then compliance officer or financial crime consultant.",v:"CAMS certification worth it AML career path"}
 ],
 ill:{type:"flow",steps:[["Onboard","KYC/CDD, risk rating"],["Screen","Sanctions, PEP, adverse media"],["Monitor","Rules flag unusual activity"],["Investigate","Context, narrative, decision"],["Report","STR to FIU-IND; never tip off"],["Refresh","Periodic KYC updation"]]},
 illCap:"The financial-crime control chain, from onboarding to periodic refresh.",
 case:{t:"A mule account at a payments start-up",b:"Monitoring flags a newly opened wallet receiving many small UPI credits from unrelated people and sending most of it out within hours. The analyst checks KYC (student, no declared income), finds two credits referencing 'refund' scams in adverse media, documents the pattern, freezes outgoing transfers per policy, and escalates to the principal officer, who files an STR. The customer is not told why."},
 ben:["Large, steady hiring at entry level across banks, GCCs and fintechs.","Builds directly on your FCRA and documentation experience."],
 trap:["Clearing screening hits without documenting the identifiers used.","Writing narratives that describe activity but not why it is unusual.","Telling a customer that a report has been filed (tipping off)."],
 quiz:[
  {q:"Moving funds through many transfers to hide their origin is called:",o:["Placement","Layering","Integration","Onboarding"],a:1,w:"Layering obscures the trail."},
  {q:"Since March 2023, the beneficial-ownership threshold for companies under India's PML rules is:",o:["25%","15%","10%","51%"],a:2,w:"It was lowered from 25% to 10%."},
  {q:"After filing an STR, the analyst should:",o:["Inform the customer politely","Not tip off the customer","Close the account publicly","Post on social media"],a:1,w:"Tipping off is prohibited."}],
 refs:[["FIU-IND (Financial Intelligence Unit – India)","https://fiuindia.gov.in/","primary"],["RBI notifications and Master Directions","https://www.rbi.org.in/","primary"],["FATF Recommendations","https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/Fatf-recommendations.html","primary"],["ACAMS certifications","https://www.acams.org/","course"],["Fox Mandal: PMLA third amendment rules (2023)","https://foxmandal.in/News/pmla-third-amendment-rules-key-highlights/","guide"]],
 terms:{"PMLA":"Prevention of Money-laundering Act, 2002.","CDD":"Customer due diligence.","EDD":"Enhanced due diligence for higher-risk customers.","PEP":"Politically exposed person.","STR":"Suspicious transaction report to FIU-IND.","CKYCR":"Central KYC Records Registry.","mule account":"An account used to receive and pass on illicit funds.","tipping off":"Telling someone they are suspected or reported."}},

{id:"clm",track:"legal",title:"Contract management & legal operations",short:"How companies draft, negotiate, sign and track contracts, and the tools that run it",weeks:"Spec 2",sk:["clm","contracts","drafting"],
 obj:"Manage the contract lifecycle, review technology and data contracts against a playbook, understand e-contracts in India, and run obligations, renewals and legal-ops metrics in a CLM tool.",
 why:"Contract analysts, legal associates on CLM template teams (Icertis, for example, has hired law graduates with 0–2 years' experience) and legal operations analysts are natural entry points for a law graduate who knows privacy. Every vendor and customer contract now carries data protection terms, so contract work and privacy work overlap.",
 subs:[
  {t:"The contract lifecycle",l:1,b:"Request and intake, drafting from approved templates, negotiation (redlines), internal approval, signature, obligations and performance tracking, renewal or expiry, and archive. Bottlenecks usually sit in intake (missing information) and approvals.",v:"contract lifecycle management stages explained"},
  {t:"Anatomy of a technology contract",l:1,b:"A master agreement (MSA) sets general terms; order forms or SOWs add the specifics; an NDA protects confidential information; a DPA sets data protection duties; an SLA sets service levels and credits. Read definitions first: they change the meaning of everything else.",v:"SaaS agreement structure MSA order form SLA DPA explained"},
  {t:"Clauses that carry risk",l:1,b:"Limitation of liability (caps and what's excluded), indemnities (Section 124 of the Indian Contract Act), damages and liquidated damages (Sections 73–74), intellectual property ownership, confidentiality, termination rights, audit rights, governing law and dispute resolution (often arbitration under the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996).",v:"limitation of liability indemnity clause explained India"},
  {t:"Playbooks and clause libraries",l:1,b:"A playbook gives, for each clause, the standard position, acceptable fallbacks, walk-away points and who can approve deviations. A clause library holds approved wording. Playbooks let junior reviewers handle most contracts and escalate only real deviations.",ex:"Liability cap: standard 12 months' fees; fallback 24 months for data breaches; walk-away: unlimited liability; approver: General Counsel.",v:"contract playbook example fallback positions"},
  {t:"Data protection clauses",l:1,b:"A DPA covers processing only on instructions, sub-processor approval, security measures, breach notice timelines, assistance with rights requests, audits, return or deletion at the end, and international transfers (GDPR standard contractual clauses; under DPDP, transfers are allowed except to countries the government restricts).",v:"data processing agreement key clauses explained"},
  {t:"E-contracts and e-signatures in India",l:2,b:"Section 10A of the IT Act recognises contracts formed electronically, and electronic signatures (including Aadhaar eSign) are recognised. Stamp duty still applies, so e-stamping matters. Some documents are excluded from the IT Act's e-record rules, including negotiable instruments other than cheques, powers of attorney, trusts, wills and contracts for sale or conveyance of immovable property.",v:"e-contracts India IT Act section 10A e-sign e-stamping"},
  {t:"CLM tools",l:2,b:"Contract lifecycle management platforms (Icertis, DocuSign CLM, Ironclad, Agiloft, Conga, and India's SpotDraft) provide templates, intake forms, approval workflows, e-signature, a searchable repository, AI clause extraction and obligation tracking. Implementations need clean templates and metadata, which is why CLM firms hire law graduates.",v:"what is CLM software demo"},
  {t:"Obligations, renewals and notice periods",l:2,b:"After signature, the value is in performance: SLAs, reporting duties, audit rights, price escalators, auto-renewals and notice periods. An obligation register with owners and reminders prevents missed renewals and penalties.",v:"contract obligation management auto renewal tracking"},
  {t:"Legal operations metrics",l:3,b:"Contract cycle time, volume by type, playbook deviation rate, turnaround by stage, outside counsel spend, and e-billing (LEDES formats). Legal ops teams use these numbers to justify tools and staffing.",v:"legal operations metrics KPIs"},
  {t:"Certifications and careers",l:3,b:"World Commerce & Contracting offers Commercial & Contract Management certifications at Associate, Practitioner (CCMP), Advanced Practitioner and Expert levels. Roles: contract analyst, contract manager, legal operations analyst, CLM implementation consultant and template specialist.",v:"WorldCC CCMP certification contract manager career"}
 ],
 ill:{type:"flow",steps:[["Intake","Request form, key data"],["Draft","Template + clause library"],["Negotiate","Redlines vs playbook"],["Approve","Deviation approvals"],["Sign","E-signature, e-stamp"],["Perform","Obligations, SLAs"],["Renew","Notice periods, renegotiate"]]},
 illCap:"The contract lifecycle. CLM tools automate each step and keep the evidence.",
 case:{t:"A missed auto-renewal",b:"A company's SaaS contract renewed automatically for three years because nobody tracked the 90-day notice period. Legal ops loads all active contracts into the CLM tool, extracts renewal dates and notice periods, assigns owners, and sets reminders 120 and 60 days before each deadline. The next year, two unwanted renewals are cancelled in time."},
 ben:["Directly uses your law degree.","Privacy-literate contract reviewers are in demand because every contract now has data terms."],
 trap:["Reviewing clauses without reading the definitions.","Accepting 'industry standard' as a reason without checking the playbook.","Forgetting stamp duty on e-signed contracts."],
 quiz:[
  {q:"Which section of the IT Act recognises electronically formed contracts?",o:["Section 43A","Section 10A","Section 66","Section 79"],a:1,w:"Section 10A."},
  {q:"A playbook's fallback position is:",o:["The first draft","An acceptable alternative to the standard position","A walk-away point","A signature block"],a:1,w:"Fallbacks are pre-approved alternatives."},
  {q:"Missing a notice period usually causes:",o:["Unwanted automatic renewal","Faster signature","Lower stamp duty","A DPA"],a:0,w:"Auto-renewals lock in terms."}],
 refs:[["India Code (Indian Contract Act, IT Act)","https://www.indiacode.nic.in/","primary"],["WorldCC Commercial & Contract Management certifications","https://www.worldcc.com/education-training/certifications/ccm.html","course"],["Icertis (CLM)","https://www.icertis.com/","guide"],["SpotDraft (CLM, India)","https://www.spotdraft.com/","guide"]],
 terms:{"CLM":"Contract lifecycle management.","redline":"Tracked changes proposed by one side.","playbook":"Rules for acceptable positions on each clause.","DPA":"Data processing agreement.","auto-renewal":"A contract that renews unless notice is given.","LEDES":"A standard format for legal e-billing."}},

{id:"ediscovery",track:"legal",title:"eDiscovery & legal technology",short:"Finding, preserving, reviewing and producing electronic evidence for litigation and investigations",weeks:"Spec 3",sk:["ediscovery","itbasics"],
 obj:"Explain the eDiscovery (EDRM) process, preserve and process data defensibly, run first-level and privilege review, build search term reports, understand analytics and AI-assisted review, and prepare productions, including cross-border privacy limits and India's electronic evidence rules.",
 why:"India hosts large eDiscovery and document review teams at legal process outsourcers and the Big 4 forensic practices (for example, Deloitte's Gurugram eDiscovery team and Kroll's Bangalore discovery team), serving US, UK and European matters. Postings ask for review workflows, privilege review, QC, Relativity exposure and attention to detail, and they hire law and legal-studies graduates. Your Provana redaction and file-QA work is directly relevant.",
 subs:[
  {t:"What eDiscovery is",l:1,b:"Electronic discovery is the process of identifying, preserving, collecting, processing, reviewing and producing electronically stored information (ESI: email, chats, documents, databases) for litigation, regulatory investigations and internal investigations. Most volume comes from US litigation, where parties must exchange relevant documents.",v:"what is eDiscovery explained"},
  {t:"The EDRM model",l:1,b:"The Electronic Discovery Reference Model shows the stages: information governance, identification, preservation, collection, processing, review, analysis, production and presentation. Volume shrinks at each stage as irrelevant data is filtered out.",v:"EDRM model explained stages"},
  {t:"Legal holds and preservation",l:1,b:"Once litigation is reasonably anticipated, relevant data must be preserved. A legal hold notice goes to custodians (people holding data), and systems are placed on hold (for example, Microsoft Purview holds). In US courts, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 37(e) allows measures when ESI that should have been preserved is lost.",v:"legal hold process eDiscovery custodians"},
  {t:"Collection, processing and chain of custody",l:1,b:"Data is collected forensically and each item's hash value (such as MD5 or SHA-256) proves it hasn't changed. Processing extracts text and metadata, removes known system files (de-NISTing), deduplicates, threads emails and runs OCR. A chain of custody records every hand-off.",v:"eDiscovery processing deduplication de-NIST chain of custody"},
  {t:"Document review",l:1,b:"First-level reviewers code documents for responsiveness, privilege and confidentiality using a coding panel and a review protocol; second-level and QC reviewers check samples and difficult calls. Accuracy and consistency matter more than speed, but both are measured (documents per hour and overturn rates).",ex:"Protocol: responsive if it discusses the 2024 pricing change; privileged if a lawyer gives legal advice; confidential if it contains customer data.",v:"first level document review responsiveness privilege coding"},
  {t:"Search terms, analytics and AI-assisted review",l:2,b:"Boolean search terms narrow the data; a search term report shows hits and unique hits per term so parties can negotiate. Analytics cluster similar documents and find near-duplicates. Technology-assisted review (TAR) and continuous active learning rank documents by likely relevance; generative-AI review tools (such as Relativity aiR) are now used, validated by statistical sampling (recall, precision, elusion tests).",v:"search term report eDiscovery TAR continuous active learning"},
  {t:"Privilege, redaction and clawback",l:2,b:"Privileged documents are withheld or redacted and listed on a privilege log (date, author, recipients, basis). Personal data may also be redacted. In US cases, Federal Rule of Evidence 502(d) orders let a party claw back privileged documents produced by mistake.",v:"privilege log redaction FRE 502(d) clawback"},
  {t:"Production",l:2,b:"Documents are produced in agreed formats (native files, or TIFF images with extracted text and load files), stamped with Bates numbers, and logged. QC checks that privileged items are excluded, redactions burned in, and metadata fields correct.",v:"eDiscovery production load files Bates numbering"},
  {t:"Cross-border data and India's evidence law",l:2,b:"Moving personal data out of the EU or India for foreign litigation raises privacy limits (GDPR transfer rules and Article 48; DPDP duties once in force), so teams minimise, redact or review in-country. In Indian courts, the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 requires a certificate with electronic records; Section 63(4) requires hash value disclosure, which the Supreme Court upheld in May 2026.",v:"cross border discovery GDPR Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam section 63 certificate"},
  {t:"Platforms, certifications and careers",l:3,b:"Review platforms: Relativity/RelativityOne, Everlaw, DISCO, Reveal; processing: Nuix; analytics: Brainspace; plus Microsoft Purview eDiscovery. Certifications: Relativity's paths (such as Review Pro, Review Management Specialist and Relativity Certified Administrator) and ACEDS CEDS. Roles: document reviewer, eDiscovery analyst, project manager, forensic consultant.",v:"Relativity certification path eDiscovery career India"}
 ],
 ill:{type:"flow",steps:[["Identify","Custodians and sources"],["Preserve","Legal hold"],["Collect","Forensic copy + hash"],["Process","Dedupe, OCR, metadata"],["Review","Responsive, privileged"],["Produce","Bates, load files, log"]]},
 illCap:"The core of the EDRM. Each step must be defensible if challenged in court.",
 case:{t:"An offshore review for a US antitrust matter",b:"A Bengaluru team receives 1.2 million documents for 30 custodians. Processing and deduplication cut it to 410,000. Search terms negotiated with the other side reduce it to 160,000, and continuous active learning prioritises the likely relevant documents. Twenty reviewers code for responsiveness and privilege under a protocol; QC samples 5% daily. Privileged documents go to a log, and production goes out in TIFF with load files and Bates numbers."},
 ben:["Steady hiring of law graduates in India's legal process outsourcing and forensic teams.","Builds on your redaction and QA experience at Provana."],
 trap:["Coding inconsistently because the protocol wasn't read carefully.","Producing privileged documents by skipping QC.","Transferring personal data abroad without checking privacy rules."],
 quiz:[
  {q:"What proves a collected file has not changed?",o:["Its file name","Its hash value","Its colour","The reviewer's memory"],a:1,w:"Hash values are digital fingerprints."},
  {q:"A privilege log lists:",o:["Responsive documents","Documents withheld or redacted for privilege, with the basis","All custodians' salaries","Search terms only"],a:1,w:"It lets the other side assess privilege claims."},
  {q:"In the EDRM, legal hold belongs to which stage?",o:["Production","Preservation","Presentation","Processing"],a:1,w:"Preservation."}],
 refs:[["EDRM (Electronic Discovery Reference Model)","https://edrm.net/","primary"],["Relativity certification paths","https://relativity.com/resources/certification/continue-your-journey/","course"],["ACEDS CEDS handbook","https://aceds.org/ceds-certification-handbook","course"],["SCC Times: Supreme Court on Section 63(4) BSA hash value certificate","https://www.scconline.com/blog/post/2026/07/08/sc-upholds-admissibility-of-electronic-evidence-under-section-63-4-bsa/","guide"]],
 terms:{"ESI":"Electronically stored information.","custodian":"A person whose data may be relevant.","legal hold":"An instruction to preserve relevant data.","de-NIST":"Removing known system files using a NIST list.","TAR":"Technology-assisted review.","Bates number":"A unique page or document number stamped on productions.","privilege":"Legal protection for lawyer–client advice and work product."}},

{id:"tns",track:"legal",title:"Trust & safety and tech policy",short:"How platforms write and enforce content rules, and the Indian, EU and US laws behind them",weeks:"Spec 4",sk:["tns","drafting"],
 obj:"Explain how trust and safety teams write and enforce platform policies, apply India's IT Act and IT Rules (including the 2025 and 2026 amendments), the EU Digital Services Act and US rules such as the TAKE IT DOWN Act, and measure enforcement quality.",
 why:"Trust and safety analysts and policy specialists work at platforms (Google, Meta, Microsoft) and at outsourcing partners (Accenture, Genpact and others) in Hyderabad, Bengaluru and Gurugram. Postings ask for policy interpretation, escalations, objectivity, resilience and regional context; senior policy roles prefer legal or policy backgrounds. India's fast-changing intermediary rules make legal training valuable.",
 subs:[
  {t:"What trust and safety teams do",l:1,b:"They protect users from harmful content and behaviour: writing policies, enforcing them through human review and automated classifiers, handling escalations and appeals, responding to government and law-enforcement requests, publishing transparency reports and reviewing new products for abuse risks.",v:"what is trust and safety explained careers"},
  {t:"Designing content policy",l:1,b:"Policies define harm categories (child safety, violent extremism, hate, harassment, non-consensual intimate imagery, scams, misinformation, IP), severity tiers and an enforcement ladder (label, reduce reach, remove, suspend, ban). Good policies are specific enough for consistent decisions, include exceptions (news, education, satire) and give examples.",ex:"Harassment policy: remove targeted insults about protected characteristics; allow criticism of public figures' actions; repeat violations lead to suspension.",v:"how content moderation policy is written"},
  {t:"India: safe harbour and the IT Rules, 2021",l:1,b:"Section 79 of the IT Act protects intermediaries from liability for user content if they follow due diligence. The IT Rules, 2021 require a grievance officer, published rules, action on court and government orders, and extra duties for significant social media intermediaries (more than 50 lakh registered users): a Chief Compliance Officer, nodal contact person, resident grievance officer and monthly compliance reports. Grievance Appellate Committees hear user appeals.",v:"IT Rules 2021 intermediary guidelines explained"},
  {t:"India's 2025 and 2026 amendments",l:1,b:"From 15 November 2025, takedown notices under Rule 3(1)(d) may only come from senior officers (Joint Secretary level, or a specially authorised DIG for police) and must state the legal basis, with monthly review. Amendments notified on 10 February 2026 (in force 20 February 2026) require labelling and provenance metadata for synthetically generated content, user declarations on significant platforms, and action on government or court orders within three hours instead of 36. The Karnataka High Court upheld the government's Sahyog takedown portal on 24 September 2025; X said it would appeal.",v:"IT Rules amendment 2026 synthetic content three hour takedown explained"},
  {t:"EU Digital Services Act",l:2,b:"The DSA requires notice-and-action systems, reasons for moderation decisions, trusted flaggers, appeals, transparency reports and, for very large online platforms, systemic risk assessments and audits. Its first non-compliance decision fined X €120 million on 5 December 2025 over its paid blue checkmark, ad repository and researcher data access.",v:"Digital Services Act explained notice and action VLOP"},
  {t:"US: Section 230, TAKE IT DOWN and child safety",l:2,b:"Section 230 broadly shields platforms from liability for user content. The TAKE IT DOWN Act requires covered platforms to remove non-consensual intimate images within 48 hours of a valid request; compliance began on 19 May 2026 and the FTC enforces it. US providers must report apparent child sexual abuse material to NCMEC's CyberTipline.",v:"TAKE IT DOWN Act explained platforms 48 hours"},
  {t:"Moderation operations and quality",l:2,b:"Work flows through queues with service levels; quality teams audit decisions; appeals are measured by overturn rate. Platform metrics include prevalence (how much violating content is seen), actioned content, proactive detection rate, and precision and recall of classifiers. Exposure to graphic content is real, so wellbeing support is part of the job.",v:"content moderation quality metrics precision recall prevalence"},
  {t:"Children's safety and data",l:2,b:"India's DPDP Act requires verifiable parental consent before processing a child's data and bars tracking, behavioural monitoring and targeted advertising directed at children (once those obligations commence). The October 2026 Removal of Difficulties Order clarified the consent wording for children and persons with disability who have lawful guardians. Platforms also use age assurance and default safety settings for teens.",v:"children online safety age assurance DPDP parental consent"},
  {t:"AI and trust and safety",l:3,b:"Deepfakes, AI-generated scams and synthetic child abuse imagery are fast-growing risks. Responses include labelling and provenance standards (such as C2PA), classifier detection and policy updates. The EU AI Act's transparency duties (Article 50) apply from 2 August 2026, with the GPAI synthetic-content duty under Article 50(2) postponed to 2 December 2026.",v:"deepfake detection provenance C2PA trust and safety"},
  {t:"Careers and skills",l:3,b:"Paths: content reviewer, trust and safety analyst, policy specialist, policy manager, or government affairs. Useful skills: precise policy writing, data analysis (Sheets, SQL basics), escalation judgment, cultural and language context, and resilience. The Trust & Safety Professional Association publishes a free curriculum.",v:"trust and safety career path policy specialist"}
 ],
 ill:{type:"flow",steps:[["Policy","Rules, severity, examples"],["Detect","User reports + classifiers"],["Review","Human decision vs policy"],["Enforce","Label, remove, suspend"],["Appeal","Second look, overturn rate"],["Report","Transparency, regulators"]]},
 illCap:"The trust and safety loop. Legal deadlines (India's 3-hour orders, the US 48-hour NCII rule) sit inside 'Enforce'.",
 case:{t:"A deepfake of a public official",b:"A significant social media intermediary receives a government order to remove a synthetic video falsely showing an official taking a bribe. The policy team confirms the order comes from an authorised senior officer, removes the content within three hours, preserves records, labels similar uploads as synthetic, and reviews whether its upload declarations caught the content. The case goes into the monthly compliance report."},
 ben:["Large platform and vendor hiring in Indian cities.","Legal reading of fast-changing rules is a real edge in policy roles."],
 trap:["Applying personal views instead of the written policy.","Missing statutory clocks (three hours in India, 48 hours under TAKE IT DOWN).","Ignoring regional language and cultural context."],
 quiz:[
  {q:"Under India's 2026 IT Rules amendments, government or court takedown orders must be acted on within:",o:["36 hours","24 hours","3 hours","7 days"],a:2,w:"Three hours, reduced from 36."},
  {q:"The DSA's first non-compliance fine (December 2025) was against:",o:["Meta","X","TikTok","Google"],a:1,w:"X, €120 million."},
  {q:"A significant social media intermediary in India has more than:",o:["5 lakh users","50 lakh registered users","5 crore users","1 lakh users"],a:1,w:"More than 50 lakh (5 million) registered users."}],
 refs:[["MeitY: IT Rules and amendments","https://www.meity.gov.in/","primary"],["Hogan Lovells: India's 2026 AI labelling and 3-hour takedown rules","https://www.hlc.com/en/publications/india-introduces-mandatory-labelling-for-ai-and-3hour-takedown-for-illegal-content","guide"],["eucrim: EU fines X €120 million under the DSA","https://eucrim.eu/news/eu-fines-x-120-million-in-first-dsa-non-compliance-decision/","guide"],["WilmerHale: The TAKE IT DOWN Act goes live","https://wilmerhale.com/en/insights/client-alerts/20260615-the-take-it-down-act-goes-live","guide"],["Trust & Safety Professional Association","https://www.tspa.org/","course"]],
 terms:{"intermediary":"A platform that carries others' content, like a social network.","safe harbour":"Legal protection for intermediaries that follow due diligence (Section 79).","SSMI":"Significant social media intermediary (over 50 lakh registered users).","NCII":"Non-consensual intimate imagery.","SGI":"Synthetically generated information (AI-made or altered content).","prevalence":"How often violating content is actually seen.","VLOP":"Very large online platform under the EU DSA."}}
];
